idr_remove(&file_priv->object_idr, args->handle);
Reason:
After `idr_alloc` inserts `obj` under the new handle, there is a window where two IDR entries (`args->handle` and `handle`) point to the same object. A concurrent `gem_close` can delete the object and remove one handle (e.g., the old handle) via `idr_remove` and `drm_gem_object_put`, leaving the new handle still pointing to freed memory. Later, when the ioctl calls `idr_remove` on the old handle, it may already be gone, but the new handle is never cleared, leading to use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
idrobj = idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, NULL, handle);
if (idrobj != obj) {
    idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, idrobj, handle);
    idr_remove(&file_priv->object_idr, args->new_handle);
    spin_unlock(&file_priv->table_lock);
    ret = -ENOENT;
    goto out_unlock;
}
...
if (ret < 0) {
    spin_lock(&file_priv->table_lock);
    idr_remove(&file_priv->object_idr, handle);
    idrobj = idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, handle);
    WARN_ON(idrobj != NULL);
    spin_unlock(&file_priv->table_lock);
    goto out_unlock;
}
...
spin_lock(&file_priv->table_lock);
idr_remove(&file_priv->object_idr, args->handle);
spin_unlock(&file_priv->table_lock);
Reason:
The fix mirrors the dance used in `gem_close` and `create_tail`. After `idr_alloc` for the new handle, the handle is immediately replaced with `NULL` via `idr_replace`. This ensures that the new IDR entry does not hold a reference to the object. If a concurrent `gem_close` occurs, it will see the `NULL` entry and not double-free. Only after all prime operations succeed (or on failure after restoring the original entry) is the old handle removed via `idr_remove`. The error path also restores the original object pointer to the old handle if prime addition fails, maintaining consistency. This prevents the race by eliminating the window where two handles point to the same object without proper ownership.

